steps.body:
The post statement of a `for` loop can move by any amount. Step by 2 to visit every other element. Start at 0 for the even positions and at 1 for the odd ones.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	s := []int{10, 11, 12, 13, 14}
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println("both loops stop cleanly on an odd length:", len(s))
}
```

```go
for i := 0; i < len(s); i += 2 { // indexes 0, 2, 4, ...
}
for i := 1; i < len(s); i += 2 { // indexes 1, 3, 5, ...
}
```

Keep the condition as `i < len(s)`. The check runs before every pass, so the loop stops cleanly even when the length is odd. Testing `i != len(s)` instead fails on an odd length: `i` steps right over `len(s)`, reads past the end, and panics with an index out of range.

To skip elements by *value* rather than position, test each one and call `continue`. It jumps straight to the next pass, so the rest of the body only sees the values you keep.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	s := []int{3, -1, 4, -5, 9}
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
for _, v := range s {
	if v < 0 {
		continue // skip negatives
	}
	fmt.Println(v)
}
```

An early `continue` keeps the main work unindented, which reads better than wrapping it all in an `if`.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	nums := []int{10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16}

	fmt.Println("Even indexes:")
	for i := 0; i < len(nums); i += 2 {
		fmt.Println(nums[i])
	}

	fmt.Println("Odd values:")
	for _, n := range nums {
		if n%2 == 0 {
			continue
		}
		fmt.Println(n)
	}
}

