steps.body:
To keep only some elements, start an empty result and `append` each element that passes a test.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	nums := []int{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(evens, nums)
}
```

```go
var evens []int
fmt.Println(evens == nil, len(evens)) // true 0
for _, v := range nums {
	if v%2 == 0 {
		evens = append(evens, v)
	}
}
```

Either start works: `var out []int` gives a nil slice and `out := []int{}` gives an empty one. `append` works on both and makes room as the result grows. One loop can fill several results at once, each with its own test.

The original slice stays unchanged. You only read from it and write into separate slices, so `nums` still holds every value afterward.

Gotcha: if nothing matched, a `var` result is still nil. Nil and empty slices both print `[]` and both have length 0, but only the nil one equals `nil`. So check for no matches with `len(out) == 0`, which is true in both cases. Testing `out == nil` breaks the day someone switches the start to `[]int{}`.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	scores := []int{45, 82, 67, 90, 38, 71}

	passed := []int{}
	var failed []int
	var perfect []int
	for _, score := range scores {
		if score >= 60 {
			passed = append(passed, score)
		} else {
			failed = append(failed, score)
		}
		if score == 100 {
			perfect = append(perfect, score)
		}
	}

	fmt.Println("Passed:", passed)
	fmt.Println("Failed:", failed)
	if len(perfect) == 0 {
		fmt.Println("No perfect scores")
	} else {
		fmt.Println("Perfect:", perfect)
	}
	fmt.Println("All:", scores)
}

